État actuel du moteur
GSWL V4.2 contrôle ses sources à fréquence horaire. La fréquence de
contrôle du moteur ne doit pas être confondue avec la fréquence de
publication de chaque série salariale (mensuelle, trimestrielle,
semestrielle ou annuelle).
La politique de sélection des données reste :
source nationale officielle > source supranationale compatible > fallback ILOSTAT > dernière observation officielle conservée
Les valeurs utilisant des unités ou des définitions différentes ne
doivent jamais être comparées directement comme s'il s'agissait d'une
même série.
🇬🇧 English version
Data Engine V4.2
GSWL is an official salary-data monitoring engine. It periodically checks public sources, preserves the latest available official observation, and explicitly separates the time when the engine runs, the time when a source responds, and the time when a specific observation is actually verified.
Disclaimer — GSWL is an information, analysis, and experimental research tool and does not constitute financial advice, a recommendation, or an inducement to buy, sell, or short any financial instrument. Every investment decision remains entirely the user's responsibility. Financial markets involve the risk of partial or total loss of capital and, depending on the instrument, leverage, or short-selling structure used, losses may exceed the capital initially committed. Data, alerts, analyses, hypotheses, simulations, and historical performance presented by GSWL do not guarantee any result or future performance.

Research hypothesis: bullish wage surprise and SELL / SHORT scenario
Hypothesis
The hypothesis being investigated is:
a sufficiently strong positive wage surprise, detected quickly after its official release, may increase inflation and/or restrictive monetary-policy expectations, push bond yields higher, and create short-term downward pressure on certain rate-sensitive assets.
The relevant signal is therefore not simply “wages increased.” It is more specifically:
unexpected wage growth + a meaningful surprise versus expectations + a macroeconomic regime in which inflation/monetary policy matters to markets + a confirming reaction in interest rates.
Under this framework, GSWL can act as the detection layer. An alert is not, by itself, a SELL order.
Why the hypothesis is economically plausible
Stronger-than-expected wage growth may be interpreted as evidence of persistent labor-market pressure and, depending on the economic regime, as a potential source of cost and inflation pressure. If markets consequently revise the expected path of policy rates upward, bond yields may rise.
The proposed transmission mechanism is:
positive wage surprise
→ inflation risk / stronger economy
→ higher-for-longer rates or delayed rate cuts
→ higher yields
→ higher discount rates
→ potential pressure on rate-sensitive assets
→ SHORT opportunity to be tested
This channel may be particularly relevant to growth/long-duration equities and indices whose valuations are highly sensitive to rates. It does not imply that all equities must fall: stronger economic data may simultaneously improve earnings expectations.
Historical evidence supporting the hypothesis
1. Employment-report surprises materially move markets
The Federal Reserve Bank of New York studied market reactions to 13 macroeconomic announcements over 1998–2007. It found that only a limited number produced economically important and persistent effects, including nonfarm payrolls, with bond yields showing particularly strong reactions. Stronger-than-expected growth and inflation news tends to push bond yields and the dollar higher.
For GSWL, this matters because U.S. wage data are released as part of the Employment Situation report and contribute to the market's inflation interpretation of that report.
Source: Federal Reserve Bank of New York, How Economic News Moves Markets
https://www.newyorkfed.org/research/current_issues/ci14-6.html
2. The Federal Reserve explicitly included hourly-earnings surprises in an event study
A Board of Governors note analyzes 10-year Treasury reactions to employment reports using data from January 1993 through December 2019. It uses a 30-minute event window — 5 minutes before and 25 minutes after the release — and simultaneously includes surprises in:
- nonfarm payrolls;
- hourly earnings;
- unemployment.
This confirms that wage surprises are a relevant variable in high-frequency analysis of interest-rate reactions around employment releases.
Source: Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve System, Why Were Treasury Yields So Stable Over the Summer?
https://www.federalreserve.gov/econres/notes/feds-notes/why-were-treasury-yields-so-stable-over-the-summer-20201229.html
3. The rates-to-equities channel has historical empirical support
Bernanke and Kuttner studied monetary-policy surprises from June 1989 through December 2002. Their benchmark result associates an unexpected 25-basis-point federal-funds-rate cut with roughly a 1% increase in a broad equity index.
By economic symmetry — without assuming perfect statistical symmetry — information that causes markets to revise rate expectations upward therefore provides a plausible mechanism for downward pressure on equity valuations.
Source: Federal Reserve Bank of New York, Bernanke & Kuttner, What Explains the Stock Market's Reaction to Federal Reserve Policy?
https://www.newyorkfed.org/research/staff_reports/sr174.html
4. Recent example consistent with the inverse mechanism
The September 2026 U.S. Employment Situation provides a useful example in the opposite direction. The BLS reported average hourly earnings rising only 0.1% month over month, to $37.81/hour, and 3.0% year over year.
The soft employment/wage report reduced expectations of tighter monetary policy and major equity indices advanced during the session. This does not prove that the opposite reaction always occurs, but it is consistent with the channel being investigated: weaker employment/wage data → less restrictive policy expectations → support for equities.
Sources:
U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics, Employment Situation — September 2026
https://www.bls.gov/news.release/archives/empsit_10022026.htm
Reuters, Soft September jobs report sends markets higher, October 2, 2026
https://www.reuters.com/business/view-soft-september-jobs-report-sends-markets-higher-2026-10-02/
GSWL signal formulation to be tested
A future experimental strategy should not trigger a SHORT solely because the current wage observation increased relative to the previous observation.
The signal should ideally measure:
salary_surprise = released value - pre-release consensus
or, in standardized form:
standardized_surprise = (released value - consensus) / historical surprise volatility
The existing GSWL change measure:
change_pct = (current value - previous value) / previous value
remains informative, but is not equivalent to a market surprise.
Additional variable: pre-release consensus
GSWL should add:
consensus_value
This represents the median or consensus market estimate captured before the official release. From it, the research layer can calculate:
surprise_value = actual_value - consensus_value
surprise_pct = ((actual_value - consensus_value) / abs(consensus_value)) × 100
and, when enough historical observations exist:
standardized_surprise = surprise_value / historical standard deviation of surprises
The consensus timestamp should also be retained:
consensus_captured_at
This establishes that the expectation used by the model was known before the official release and helps prevent hindsight bias.
This distinction is essential. Wage growth of 0.4% may be materially hawkish when the market expected 0.2%, but much less informative when consensus was already 0.4% or 0.5%.
The experimental chain therefore becomes:
released value + consensus_value → surprise → yield confirmation → SHORT scenario to test
A detected signal never means that a trade will be profitable and does not transform a GSWL alert into financial advice.
Experimental SELL scenario
A more robust hypothesis would test a SHORT only when several conditions converge:
1. GSWL detects a new official wage observation.
2. Wage growth exceeds the consensus available before the release.
3. The surprise exceeds a predefined statistical threshold.
4. The relevant bond yield rises immediately after the release.
5. The move is not contradicted by a major negative payroll surprise or a significant rise in unemployment.
6. Entry is evaluated over a predefined post-alert window without changing the rules after observing the result.
The bond yield acts as confirmation of the proposed mechanism. A positive wage surprise without a rise in yields may indicate that the market had already priced it in, that another component of the report dominates, or that the macroeconomic regime has changed.
Historical windows to measure
For each historical alert, a backtest should record the asset price at publication and calculate SHORT returns at +5 minutes, +15 minutes, +30 minutes, +1 hour, market close, +1 day, +3 days, and +5 days.
The dataset should also retain the raw and standardized wage surprise, payroll and unemployment surprises, 2-year and 10-year Treasury reactions, equity-index reaction, pre-release volatility, inflation regime, monetary-policy regime, Maximum Favorable Excursion (MFE), Maximum Adverse Excursion (MAE), and net returns after spread, slippage, borrowing costs, and other applicable costs.
Hypothesis validation
The purpose of the backtest is not to select a few famous sessions that support the scenario. All available releases over a sufficiently long period should be tested.
The SELL hypothesis would gain credibility if, after costs, large positive wage surprises produce positive average SHORT returns over a predefined window; the effect becomes stronger when 2-year/10-year yields rise after the release; hit rate and expected return remain positive out of sample; results survive different rate and inflation regimes; and performance does not depend on a handful of extreme events.
Conversely, if the effect disappears out of sample, after costs, or after controlling for payroll and unemployment surprises, the hypothesis should be rejected or reformulated.
Risks and limitations
A SELL/SHORT position can incur rapid and substantial losses when the market reacts in the opposite direction. Prices can also experience slippage, gaps, extreme volatility, or sharp reversals following a release. Fees, spreads, borrowing costs, margin requirements, and leverage can materially worsen real-world results relative to a backtest.
GSWL must therefore be treated as a detection and statistical-research tool, not as a system that guarantees an entry point, return, or maximum profit. Any validation of the hypothesis must include adverse events and losses, not only historical cases that support it.
Conclusion of the directional research hypothesis
The available historical evidence provides a credible basis for researching the GSWL directional hypothesis: labor-market announcements matter for asset prices, hourly-earnings surprises are among the variables used to explain yield reactions around employment reports, and unexpected changes in monetary-policy expectations have historically had meaningful effects on equities.
This supports building and testing the chain:
positive wage surprise → higher-yield confirmation → SELL/SHORT scenario.
However, the evidence reviewed does not establish that shorting immediately after every wage increase maximizes gains. That proposition can only be evaluated using a historical database containing the official release, pre-release consensus, timestamps, interest-rate reaction, and intraday market prices.
